#!chezscheme
;;; Regenerate test/sexpr-vectors.json -- the golden wire fixture that
;;; pins rt/sexpr.mjs and lib/web/sexpr.ss to (igropyr sexpr).
;;;
;;; The fixture is COMMITTED; running the suite does not need Chez or
;;; igropyr.  This script is only for regenerating it, and it exists so
;;; that no VERDICT in the fixture is ever hand-written.  The inputs of
;;; the read probes are chosen here -- that is what a probe is -- but
;;; what the fixture records about each one, and every wire string in
;;; the write groups, comes out of the igropyr implementation, which is
;;; the format's authority.  Three implementations of one format is a twin-drift
;;; risk; a shared golden generated by one of them is how the other two
;;; are held to it, rather than to each other.
;;;
;;; Requires a sibling checkout of igropyr (the workspace layout puts it
;;; at ../../01-igropyr relative to this file) and Chez Scheme:
;;;
;;;   cd ../01-igropyr && scheme -q --libdirs . \
;;;       --script ../03-goeteia/test/gen-sexpr-vectors.sc \
;;;       ../03-goeteia/test/sexpr-vectors.json
;;;
;;; It refuses to run if it cannot see the source, if sexpr.sc is dirty,
;;; or if the sentinel below says the thing answering is older than the
;;; behaviour this fixture is supposed to describe.
;;;
;;; It writes to the path given on the command line, exactly as given
;;; -- there is no source-relative resolution, so the invocation above
;;; spells the destination out.  (This used to claim the output was
;;; placed relative to this file, which it never was.)  Entries:
;;;
;;;   write: {name, kind, wire_b64}   -- what the oracle emits for a
;;;                                      value the reader below rebuilds
;;;   read:  {name, input_b64, accepted, canonical_b64 | error}
;;;
;;; Wire text is base64-wrapped so that the fixture carries bytes rather
;;; than a second layer of escaping to get wrong -- except for entries
;;; too long to carry literally, which appear as a `*_rule` object (a
;;; prefix, a repeated character, a count, a suffix) whose two literal
;;; halves are plain JSON strings.  Those halves are short in the cases
;;; that motivated the form and long in the two that did not; see the
;;; note on wire-rule.

;;; ---------------------------------------------------------------
;;; MEASURE THE SOURCE, NOT WHATEVER IS LYING AROUND
;;;
;;; A compiled sexpr.so in the igropyr tree shadows sexpr.sc under the
;;; default library extensions, and a stale one answers for a version
;;; nobody is looking at.  That happened: a fixture was generated
;;; against an .so built weeks earlier while recording the current git
;;; HEAD beside it -- the numbers were real, the attribution was not,
;;; and nothing in the output showed the difference.  Seven guards, in
;;; order of how early they catch it:
;;;
;;;   1. resolve libraries from .sc ONLY, so the .so cannot be loaded;
;;;   2. require the library search path to be the CURRENT DIRECTORY and
;;;      nothing else -- `--libdirs /elsewhere:.` loads a substitute
;;;      while every check below still reads the normal checkout;
;;;   3. read the commit in-process, never by hand;
;;;   4. refuse to run against a dirty sexpr.sc OR crypto.sc -- base64
;;;      comes from the second one and decides half the accept/reject
;;;      boundary recorded here;
;;;   5. check that the path the IMPORT resolves through (igropyr/, a
;;;      symlink in this checkout) is byte-identical to the files those
;;;      guards inspected, so the attribution cannot describe one file
;;;      while another answers;
;;;   6. the bytes are captured BEFORE the import and re-checked after
;;;      the last measurement -- which rules out a change that PERSISTS,
;;;      not a transient swap across the import window (a stated
;;;      residual; see the note on pre-import-bytes);
;;;   7. SENTINELS: base64 must draw its line exactly where this format
;;;      says (#vu8"A" empty, #vu8"AB" refused), and the writer must
;;;      refuse the symbol 0x10 -- behaviour the authority only gained
;;;      in 08cc3f8, which is why it discriminates.  What the sentinels
;;;      establish is that the BEHAVIOUR is present, not why it is not:
;;;      an older artifact, a substituted one and a newer one that
;;;      regressed all fail the same way, and all three stop the
;;;      generator instead of becoming the golden.
;;; ---------------------------------------------------------------

(library-extensions '(".sc"))

;; The bytes are read BEFORE the authority is imported.  Reading them
;; after would leave a window: a commit landing between the import and
;; the read leaves the old code instantiated while every guard happily
;; describes the new bytes -- five checks agreeing on the wrong thing.
;; These are captured first and re-checked after the last measurement.
;;
;; What that rules out is a change that PERSISTS -- an edit, a rebuild,
;; a commit landing mid-run.  What it does not rule out is a transient
;; swap: replace the file after the capture, let the import happen, put
;; the original back before the checks, and every comparison passes on
;; bytes that are not what answered.
;;
;; The only thing that would close it is fingerprinting what Chez
;; actually instantiated -- asking the runtime, not the filesystem.
;;
;; Two remedies that read as plausible and are not, both of which stood
;; in this comment before being checked: holding the file OPEN across
;; the import (a descriptor pins an inode while the import resolves a
;; PATH, so the pathname can be repointed with the descriptor none the
;; wiser), and taking a LOCK (it would have to be respected by whoever
;; replaces the file, which is precisely the party we cannot constrain
;; -- a lock the importer honours stops nothing).  A proposed remedy is
;; a claim of the same weight as any other sentence here, and these two
;; were written casually because they were hypothetical.
;;
;; Fingerprinting the instantiated library is plating the measuring
;; instrument for a threat this script does not have: it is run by hand,
;; on one machine.  So the race is written down as a residual rather
;; than defended against.  (An earlier version of this comment claimed
;; "the window is empty at both ends", which is stronger than what the
;; check delivers.)
(import (chezscheme))

(define measured-sources '("sexpr.sc" "crypto.sc"))

(define (file-bytes path)
  (and (file-exists? path)
       (call-with-port (open-file-input-port path) get-bytevector-all)))

(define pre-import-bytes
  (map (lambda (f)
         (let ((b (file-bytes f)))
           (unless (bytevector? b)
             (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                    (string-append "cannot read " f
                                   "; run this from the igropyr checkout")))
           (cons f b)))
       measured-sources))

(import (only (igropyr sexpr) sexpr->string-extended string->sexpr-extended)
        (only (igropyr crypto) base64-encode))

;; ...and nothing may have moved underneath while we measured.
(define (sources-unchanged!)
  (for-each
   (lambda (f)
     (unless (bytevector=? (cdr (assoc f pre-import-bytes)) (file-bytes f))
       (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
              (string-append f " changed while the fixture was being "
                             "generated; nothing written"))))
   measured-sources))

(define (shell-line cmd)
  ;; the first line of a command's output ("" when it produced none)
  (let-values (((stdin stdout stderr pid)
                (open-process-ports cmd 'line (native-transcoder))))
    (let ((line (get-line stdout)))
      (close-port stdin) (close-port stdout) (close-port stderr)
      (if (eof-object? line) "" line))))

;; A command's whole output, and a flag for "it printed nothing to
;; stderr".  `git status` answering nothing looks identical to `git`
;; not running at all through shell-line, and "the tool failed" must
;; not read as "the tree is clean".
(define (shell-all cmd)
  (let-values (((stdin stdout stderr pid)
                (open-process-ports cmd 'block (native-transcoder))))
    (let loop ((out '()) (err '()))
      (let ((o (get-string-all stdout))
            (e (get-string-all stderr)))
        (close-port stdin) (close-port stdout) (close-port stderr)
        (cons (if (eof-object? o) "" o) (if (eof-object? e) "" e))))))

;; The commit of the FILE THAT WAS MEASURED, not of the checkout: the
;; tree's HEAD moves with commits that never touched this codec (a
;; test-only commit sitting on top would name a change the fixture
;; cannot possibly reflect), while sexpr.sc's last commit is exactly
;; what answered here.
(define (commit-of file)
  (let* ((r (shell-all (string-append "git log -1 --format=%H -- " file)))
         (c (let ((out (car r)))
              ;; first line, without its newline
              (let scan ((i 0))
                (cond ((= i (string-length out)) out)
                      ((char=? (string-ref out i) #\newline) (substring out 0 i))
                      (else (scan (+ i 1))))))))
    (unless (string=? (cdr r) "")
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
             (string-append "git failed while reading " file "'s commit: "
                            (cdr r))))
    (unless (= 40 (string-length c))
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
             (string-append "cannot read " file "'s commit (got "
                            (if (string=? c "") "nothing" c)
                            "); run this from the igropyr checkout")))
    c))

;; Every measured source gets its own, because they move independently:
;; a clean commit touching only crypto.sc changes what base64 accepts
;; while sexpr.sc's commit -- and so a single "authority_commit" -- stays
;; exactly the same.  One id per file, or the attribution is a guess
;; about the half of the behaviour it does not name.
(define (authority-commit) (recorded-commit "sexpr.sc"))

;; Every source the measurement depends on, not just the obvious one.
;; crypto.sc supplies base64, which decides half the accept/reject
;; boundary this fixture records; a substituted one would change the
;; golden with nothing in the output to say so.
(define measured-sources '("sexpr.sc" "crypto.sc"))

;; "Clean" is decided by CONTENT, not by what git status prints.  A file
;; marked assume-unchanged or skip-worktree carries modified bytes while
;; status stays silent, and a status command that fails to run prints
;; nothing at all -- both of which a porcelain check reads as "clean".
;; So the working file is compared against the blob of the very commit
;; the fixture is about to record.  If those differ, the id would name
;; something other than what answered, whatever git status thinks.
(define (blob-bytes commit path)
  (let* ((r (shell-all (string-append "git show " commit ":" path)))
         (out (car r)) (err (cdr r)))
    (unless (string=? err "")
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
             (string-append "cannot read " path " at " commit ": " err)))
    (string->utf8 out)))

;; Read ONCE, verified once, recorded from the same values.  Reading the
;; commit again when the fixture is written would open a window: a
;; commit landing in between gets recorded while the code that answered
;; -- imported long before -- is the old one, and the two ids would be
;; mutually consistent and wrong together.
(define authority-commit-table
  (map (lambda (f) (cons f (commit-of f))) measured-sources))

(define (recorded-commit f)
  (let ((hit (assoc f authority-commit-table)))
    (unless hit
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors (string-append "no commit read for " f)))
    (cdr hit)))

(define (authority-clean!)
  (for-each
   (lambda (f)
     (let* ((commit (recorded-commit f))
            ;; the bytes captured before the import, not a fresh read:
            ;; what answered is what must match the recorded commit
            (working (cdr (assoc f pre-import-bytes)))
            (recorded (blob-bytes commit f)))
       ;; (no bytevector? check: pre-import-bytes refuses to build
       ;; without one, so this could not fire.)
       (unless (bytevector=? working recorded)
         (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                (string-append
                 f " differs from its own recorded commit " commit
                 " -- the id in the fixture would name something other than "
                 "what was measured (note that git status can stay silent "
                 "for a file marked assume-unchanged or skip-worktree)")))))
   measured-sources))

;; ...and the file the IMPORT resolves to is the file those guards
;; inspected.  With --libdirs . the library name resolves through an
;; `igropyr/` directory, which in this checkout is a symlink; the
;; guards above read ./sexpr.sc.  Repoint that symlink and every check
;; still passes while something else answers.  So: compare the bytes.
(define (same-file? a b)
  (and (file-exists? a) (file-exists? b)
       (let ((x (call-with-port (open-file-input-port a) get-bytevector-all))
             (y (call-with-port (open-file-input-port b) get-bytevector-all)))
         (and (bytevector? x) (bytevector? y) (bytevector=? x y)))))

;; ...and the library path itself.  The guards below compare the bytes at
;; two PATHS, which says nothing about which directory Chez searched
;; first: `--libdirs /elsewhere:.` loads a substitute while every byte,
;; commit and cleanliness check still reads the normal checkout, and the
;; sentinels only cover the two behaviours they name.  Demonstrated, not
;; imagined -- which is the standard this list is extended on.
(define (library-path-is-here!)
  (let ((dirs (library-directories)))
    (unless (equal? dirs '(("." . ".")))
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
             (format "the library search path is ~s, not just the current \
                      directory -- an earlier entry can supply a different \
                      (igropyr sexpr) than the one every other guard here \
                      inspects.  Run this with --libdirs . and nothing else."
                     dirs)))))

(define (resolution-matches-guards!)
  (for-each
   (lambda (f)
     (let ((via-library-path (string-append "igropyr/" f)))
       (unless (same-file? f via-library-path)
         (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                (string-append
                 "the import resolves through " via-library-path
                 ", which is not the same bytes as " f
                 " -- the commit and cleanliness guards would be describing "
                 "a different file than the one that answered")))))
   measured-sources))

;; The sentinel.  Not a smoke test -- a refusal to produce a fixture at
;; all unless the thing that answered HAS THE BEHAVIOUR this fixture is
;; about to describe.
;;
;; That is all it establishes, and the messages below say only that.
;; They used to name a cause -- "pre-08cc3f8 behaviour", "most likely a
;; stale sexpr.so shadowing it" -- which the check cannot tell: a newer
;; source that regressed fails identically, and a shadowing .so is
;; already impossible because libraries resolve from .sc only (guard 1).
;; A failure message that names the wrong cause sends the next reader to
;; the wrong place, which is worse than naming none.
(define (sentinel!)
  ;; base64 first, because it comes from the other measured source and
  ;; the symbol sentinel would not notice a substituted crypto.sc: the
  ;; two payloads below straddle the non-canonical-tail rule that half
  ;; this fixture's read verdicts turn on.
  (let ((empty (guard (e (#t 'refused)) (string->sexpr-extended "#vu8\"A\"")))
        (nc (guard (e (#t 'refused)) (string->sexpr-extended "#vu8\"AB\""))))
    (unless (and (bytevector? empty) (= 0 (bytevector-length empty))
                 (eq? nc 'refused))
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
             "SENTINEL FAILED: base64 does not draw the line where this format\n\
              says it does (#vu8\"A\" empty, #vu8\"AB\" refused).  The\n\
              base64-decode that answered here does not have the behaviour\n\
              half this fixture's read verdicts turn on.  Whether that is a\n\
              substituted crypto.sc, an older one, or a newer one that\n\
              regressed, this check cannot say.  No fixture written.")))
  (let ((r (guard (e (#t 'refused))
             (sexpr->string-extended (string->symbol "0x10")))))
    (unless (eq? r 'refused)
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
             (string-append
              "SENTINEL FAILED: the writer emitted the symbol 0x10, which this\n\
               format's reader refuses.  The implementation that answered\n\
               here does not consult the reader's grammar before writing a\n\
               name -- behaviour this fixture is about to record as present.\n\
               Whether that is an older source, a newer one that regressed,\n\
               or something else entirely, this check cannot say.  No fixture\n\
               written.")))))

;; ---- JSON emission (only what this fixture needs) ---------------------

(define (json-string s p)
  (put-char p #\")
  (string-for-each
   (lambda (c)
     (cond ((char=? c #\") (put-string p "\\\""))
           ((char=? c #\\) (put-string p "\\\\"))
           ((char=? c #\newline) (put-string p "\\n"))
           ((char=? c #\tab) (put-string p "\\t"))
           ((char=? c #\return) (put-string p "\\r"))
           ((char<? c #\space)
            (put-string p "\\u")
            (let ((h (number->string (char->integer c) 16)))
              (do ((k (string-length h) (+ k 1))) ((= k 4))
                (put-char p #\0))
              (put-string p h)))
           (else (put-char p c))))
   s)
  (put-char p #\"))

(define (b64 s) (base64-encode (string->utf8 s)))

;; A long wire string -> (prefix repeated-char count suffix), a rule
;; that rebuilds it, for entries too big to carry literally.
;;
;; It does NOT refuse strings that are not "one character repeated
;; between a short prefix and suffix", which an earlier version of this
;; comment claimed: a 5000-character `abab...` comes back as a rule with
;; a 4999-character prefix and a count of 1, because the run scan
;; compares the last character with itself.
;;
;; Nor are the entries that reach it all genuine runs.  Two of the four
;; are degenerate in exactly that way: `list-flat-long` and
;; `bytevector-64k` come out as a long literal suffix, and a long
;; literal prefix, around a run of two or three characters.  (Lengths
;; deliberately not written down: they are a property of vectors that
;; may change, and nothing here would notice the comment going stale.)  Nothing rejects those, and
;; nothing needs to: this is a REBUILD rule, not a compression, and a
;; degenerate one rebuilds its string exactly like any other.  It buys
;; readability where a run exists and costs nothing where it does not.
;;
;; Nothing verifies the rule against the wire here; that check existed
;; and was tautological (head, run and tail are sliced out of the same
;; string).  What can actually go wrong is the EMISSION, and that is
;; covered downstream: emitting a repeat count one too high turns
;; test/sexpr-mjs.mjs red, because it builds the value by hand and
;; writes it against the rebuilt wire.  Measured, not assumed -- and the
;; Scheme golden suite stays green on the same mutation, being a fixed
;; point over those same rebuilt bytes.
(define long-wire-threshold 4096)
(define (wire-rule wire)
  (let ((n (string-length wire)))
    (and (> n long-wire-threshold)
         (let* ((head (let scan ((i 0))
                        (if (or (= i n) (char=? (string-ref wire i)
                                                (string-ref wire (min (+ i 1) (- n 1)))))
                            i
                            (scan (+ i 1)))))
                (c (string-ref wire head))
                (tail (let scan ((i head))
                        (if (and (< i n) (char=? (string-ref wire i) c))
                            (scan (+ i 1))
                            i)))
                (prefix (substring wire 0 head))
                (suffix (substring wire tail n))
                (count (- tail head))
                )
           ;; NOT checked here: `prefix + count copies of c + suffix ==
           ;; wire`.  That held by construction -- head, the run and
           ;; tail are sliced out of the same string -- so it could not
           ;; fail, and it did not cover the thing that can go wrong,
           ;; which is what gets EMITTED.  Measured: emitting a repeat
           ;; count one too high turns test/sexpr-mjs.mjs red (it builds
           ;; the value by hand and writes it against the rebuilt wire)
           ;; and leaves the Scheme golden suite green, because that one
           ;; is a fixed point over the same rebuilt bytes.
           (list prefix (string c) count suffix)))))

;; ---- the write vectors: a value, and what the oracle makes of it ------
;; Each entry is (name kind . value).  `kind` groups them so the suites
;; can assert coverage per type rather than only a total count.

(define deep-63
  ;; 63 nested lists: legal (the limit is 64), and the depth the wire
  ;; format's own parser is held to on the other side
  (let loop ((k 0) (x '()))
    (if (= k 62) x (loop (+ k 1) (list x)))))

(define write-cases
  (list
   (list "int-zero" "integer" 0)
   (list "int-neg-one" "integer" -1)
   (list "int-42" "integer" 42)
   (list "int-2^62" "integer" (expt 2 62))
   (list "int-neg-2^90" "integer" (- (expt 2 90)))
   (list "ratio-neg-seven-halves" "ratio" -7/2)
   (list "ratio-355-113" "ratio" 355/113)
   (list "string-empty" "string" "")
   (list "string-quote-backslash" "string" "a\"b\\c")
   (list "string-literal-newline" "string" "line1\nline2")
   (list "string-tab" "string" "a\tb")
   (list "string-unicode" "string" "\x4e2d;\x6587;\x1F600;")
   (list "symbol-ok" "symbol" 'ok)
   (list "symbol-get-user" "symbol" 'get-user)
   (list "symbol-punctuation" "symbol" (string->symbol "a*-+<=>?!b"))
   (list "bool-true" "boolean" #t)
   (list "bool-false" "boolean" #f)
   (list "empty-list" "list" '())
   (list "list-proper" "list" '(1 2 3))
   (list "list-dotted" "list" '(1 . 2))
   (list "list-dotted-tail" "list" '(1 2 . 3))
   (list "list-alist" "list" '((a . 1) (b . 2)))
   (list "list-nested" "list" '(1 (2 (3 4)) 5))
   (list "list-mixed" "list" (list 'get-user 42 "x" #t))
   (list "vector-empty" "vector" (vector))
   (list "vector-nested-dotted" "vector" (vector 1 (cons 2 3)))
   (list "bytevector-empty" "bytevector" (bytevector))
   (list "bytevector-0-1-255" "bytevector" (bytevector 0 1 255))
   (list "bytevector-32" "bytevector"
         (u8-list->bytevector
          (let loop ((k 0) (acc '()))
            (if (= k 32) (reverse acc) (loop (+ k 1) (cons (* k 7) acc))))))
   (list "flonum-1.5" "flonum" 1.5)
   (list "flonum-1.0" "flonum" 1.0)
   (list "flonum-0.1" "flonum" 0.1)
   (list "flonum-inf" "flonum" +inf.0)
   (list "flonum-neg-inf" "flonum" -inf.0)
   (list "flonum-nan" "flonum" +nan.0)
   (list "flonum-neg-zero" "flonum" -0.0)
   (list "deep-63" "depth" deep-63)))

;; ---- the read probes: the oracle decides accept vs reject ------------
;; Nothing here is judged by hand: whatever igropyr does with each of
;; these IS the specification, including the cases where that is a
;; refusal and the cases where it accepts something surprising.

(define read-cases
  (list
   (list "read-int" "1")
   (list "read-ratio-reduces" "4/2")
   (list "read-ratio-zero-den" "1/0")
   (list "read-bare-flonum-text" "1.5")
   (list "read-exponent-text" "1e9")
   (list "read-symbol-dot" "(. a)")
   (list "read-dotted-two-tails" "(a . b c)")
   (list "read-vector-dotted" "#(1 . 2)")
   (list "read-unterminated-string" "\"abc")
   (list "read-stray-close" ")")
   (list "read-trailing-datum" "1 2")
   (list "read-trailing-space" "1   ")
   (list "read-whitespace-variants" "(1\t2\r\n3)")
   (list "read-escapes-n-t-r" "\"a\\nb\\tc\\rd\"")
   (list "read-escape-bad" "\"a\\qb\"")
   (list "read-dangling-escape" "\"a\\")
   (list "read-hash-bare" "#")
   (list "read-hash-t-glued" "#tx")
   (list "read-hash-t-delim" "(#t)")
   (list "read-false-vs-f8" "#f")
   (list "read-f8-short-b64" "#f8\"A\"")
   (list "read-f8-wrong-length" "#f8\"AAAA\"")
   (list "read-f8-bad-char" "#f8\"AAAAAAAAAAA!\"")
   (list "read-bv-bad-padding" "#vu8\"A\"")
   (list "read-bv-unterminated" "#vu8\"AAAA")
   (list "read-vu8-truncated-tag" "#vu")
   (list "read-empty-input" "")
   (list "read-only-space" "   ")
   ;; N nested lists with an EMPTY LIST innermost, and the name says N.
   ;; They used to wrap an already-nested `()` N times and call that
   ;; depth N, so read-deep-65 was 66 levels -- the verdicts were the
   ;; authority's and right, the names were off by one, and nothing
   ;; asserted them.
   ;;
   ;; Named "-empty" because the innermost datum CHANGES THE ANSWER, and
   ;; two probes at the same paren count with opposite verdicts should
   ;; not have near-identical names.  Measured: 65 levels ending in `()`
   ;; is ACCEPTED while 65 levels ending in `1` is REFUSED, because the
   ;; empty list is recognised where an atom costs one more descent.
   ;; The atom pair below is the one that sits on the limit.
   (list "read-deep-65-empty"
         (let loop ((k 1) (s "()"))
           (if (= k 65) s (loop (+ k 1) (string-append "(" s ")")))))
   (list "read-deep-63-empty"
         (let loop ((k 1) (s "()"))
           (if (= k 63) s (loop (+ k 1) (string-append "(" s ")")))))
   (list "read-long-token"
         (let loop ((k 0) (acc '()))
           (if (= k 70000)
               (list->string acc)
               (loop (+ k 1) (cons #\a acc)))))
   (list "read-symbol-all-punct" "*-+<=>?!._%&^~:@")
   (list "read-neg-zero-int" "-0")
   (list "read-plus-int" "+1")
   ;; tokens whose SHAPE starts numeric but whose text is not a number
   ;; in this grammar: whether they read back at all decides whether a
   ;; symbol of that name may be written
   (list "read-hex-shaped" "0x10")
   (list "read-exp-shaped" "1e3")
   (list "read-digit-then-alpha" "12abc")
   (list "read-dot-alone" ".")
   ;; where exactly the base64 decoder draws its line: the JS side has
   ;; to refuse and accept in the same places, and "non-canonical tail"
   ;; is not a boundary anyone should guess at
   (list "read-bv-noncanonical-tail" "#vu8\"AR==\"")
   (list "read-bv-two-chars" "#vu8\"AB\"")
   (list "read-bv-all-padding" "#vu8\"====\"")
   (list "read-bv-valid-one-byte" "#vu8\"/w==\"")
   (list "read-bv-padding-midway" "#vu8\"A=A=\"")
   (list "read-f8-noncanonical" "#f8\"AAAAAAAAAAB=\"")
   (list "read-f8-nine-bytes" "#f8\"AAAAAAAAAAAA\"")
   ;; the off-by-one pairs: 64 levels legal / 65 not, and a token at
   ;; the cap / one past it, for a symbol AND for a number (a cap that
   ;; only counts symbols lets a huge digit run through)
   (list "read-deep-64-atom"
         (let loop ((k 0) (s "1"))
           (if (= k 64) s (loop (+ k 1) (string-append "(" s ")")))))
   (list "read-deep-65-atom"
         (let loop ((k 0) (s "1"))
           (if (= k 65) s (loop (+ k 1) (string-append "(" s ")")))))
   (list "read-token-65536" (make-string 65536 #\a))
   (list "read-token-65537" (make-string 65537 #\a))
   (list "read-number-token-65536" (make-string 65536 #\1))
   (list "read-number-token-65537" (make-string 65537 #\1))
   ;; a position AFTER an astral character: counted in code points by
   ;; the authority, in UTF-16 units by a naive JS port
   (list "read-astral-then-error" "(\x1F600; . b c)")
   (list "read-astral-trailing" "\"\x1F600;\" x")
   ;; trailing data: the position is where the second datum starts to
   ;; matter, not where it ends
   (list "read-trailing-wide-gap" "1   2")
   ;; the strict/extended distinction, which a dispatcher could get
   ;; wrong without any of the above noticing
   (list "read-f8-no-quote" "#f8x")
   (list "read-false-then-delim" "(#f)")
   ;; number-grammar semantics, pinned as VALUES not just verdicts
   (list "read-plus-five" "+5")
   (list "read-leading-zeros" "007")
   (list "read-neg-leading-zeros" "-007")
   (list "read-ratio-2-4" "2/4")
   (list "read-ratio-0-7" "0/7")
   (list "read-ratio-neg-den" "1/-2")
   (list "read-double-slash" "1//2")))

;; ---- write-reject probes: names the writer must refuse --------------
;; A symbol whose name reads back as a different datum cannot cross this
;; grammar (there is no |escaped| symbol form), so the writer refuses it
;; rather than let it deform in transit.  Which names those are is the
;; oracle's call, not this file's: each is recorded with what actually
;; happened.

(define write-reject-cases
  ;; A SYSTEMATIC MATRIX, not a hand-picked list.  Hand-picked names
  ;; gave a covering illusion once already: 61 of them agreed with the
  ;; authority while a whole family -- the complex forms +i, +2i,
  ;; +inf.0i, +1@2 -- disagreed, because nobody had thought to write
  ;; one down.  The cross product below is mechanical, so what it
  ;; misses is a shape nobody has named rather than a shape someone
  ;; forgot.  The authority answers each one and the table IS the
  ;; acceptance surface; the implementations may match it however they
  ;; like.
  (let* ((signs (list "" "+" "-"))
         (magnitudes
          (list "" "1" "12" "0" "007" "1.5" "1." ".5" "1e3" "1E3" "1e+3"
                "1/2" "1/0" "1/" "2/4" "1/-2" "1/+2" "-1/2" "1//2"
                "1E+3"))
         ;; Every alphabetic part of this grammar is case-insensitive,
         ;; and sampling a few spellings is how the last two gaps got
         ;; in.  So the casings are GENERATED: all of them, for the
         ;; exponent markers and for both specials.
         (case-variants
          (lambda (word)
            (let loop ((i 0) (acc (list "")))
              (if (= i (string-length word))
                  (map (lambda (r) (list->string (reverse (string->list r)))) acc)
                  (let ((c (string-ref word i)))
                    (loop (+ i 1)
                          (let inner ((as acc) (out '()))
                            (if (null? as)
                                out
                                (inner (cdr as)
                                       (cons (string-append (string (char-upcase c)) (car as))
                                             (cons (string-append (string c) (car as))
                                                   out)))))))))))
         (magnitudes
          (append magnitudes
                  (let loop ((ms (list #\e #\s #\f #\d #\l)) (acc '()))
                    (if (null? ms)
                        acc
                        (loop (cdr ms)
                              (cons (string-append "1" (string (car ms)) "3")
                                    (cons (string-append "1" (string (char-upcase (car ms))) "3")
                                          acc)))))
                  (case-variants "inf.0")
                  (case-variants "nan.0")))
         ;; both casings: the suffix is case-insensitive to the host
         ;; reader, and a matrix that only ever wrote `i` would leave a
         ;; predicate that only accepts `i` looking correct
         (suffixes (list "" "i" "I"))
         (matrix
          (let loop ((ss signs) (acc '()))
            (if (null? ss)
                (reverse acc)
                (loop (cdr ss)
                      (let inner ((ms magnitudes) (acc acc))
                        (if (null? ms)
                            acc
                            (let suf ((xs suffixes) (acc acc))
                              (if (null? xs)
                                  (inner (cdr ms) acc)
                                  (suf (cdr xs)
                                       (cons (string-append (car ss) (car ms) (car xs))
                                             acc))))))))))
         ;; the two-part forms: a real, then an imaginary or a polar angle
         ;; Both operands of a compound form are drawn from a list that
         ;; spans the families -- decimals, ratios, exponents, specials
         ;; -- rather than decimals alone, because a predicate can
         ;; handle each family standing alone and still fail on their
         ;; composition (`+1e3+2i`, `+1/2@3/4`).  It is a spanning
         ;; sample, not the full cross product of the magnitude set:
         ;; that would multiply the corpus by two orders of magnitude
         ;; for shapes that differ only in which digits are present.
         (compound
          (let loop ((as (list "" "1" "1.5" "inf.0" "1/2" "1e3" "007" ".5"))
                     (acc '()))
            (if (null? as)
                (reverse acc)
                (loop (cdr as)
                      (append
                       (list (string-append (car as) "+2i")
                             (string-append (car as) "+2I")
                             (string-append (car as) "-2i")
                             (string-append (car as) "-2I")
                             (string-append (car as) "+i")
                             (string-append (car as) "+I")
                             (string-append (car as) "+inf.0i")
                             (string-append (car as) "+INF.0I")
                             (string-append (car as) "+Inf.0i"))
                       (list (string-append (car as) "+3/4i")
                             (string-append (car as) "+1e3i")
                             (string-append (car as) "+2s3i"))
                       ;; the right operand is a REAL, sign included:
                       ;; a corpus of unsigned right operands would let
                       ;; a predicate that only accepts unsigned ones
                       ;; look correct, and `+1@-2` is a number to the
                       ;; host reader
                       (if (string=? (car as) "")
                           '()
                           (let right ((rs (list "2" "-2" "+2" "inf.0"
                                                 "-inf.0" "3/4" "-3/4"
                                                 "2s3" "-2s3" "+inf.0"))
                                       (acc '()))
                             (if (null? rs)
                                 acc
                                 (right (cdr rs)
                                        (cons (string-append (car as) "@" (car rs))
                                              (cons (string-append "+" (car as) "@" (car rs))
                                                    acc))))))
                       acc)))))
         ;; every compound form also with a leading sign: the sign is a
         ;; dimension of its own and the last three gaps were all a
         ;; dimension that existed in one position and not another
         (compound (append compound
                           (map (lambda (x) (string-append "+" x)) compound)
                           (map (lambda (x) (string-append "-" x)) compound)))
         ;; names that have nothing to do with numbers, kept so the
         ;; character rules and the length rule stay covered too
         (others
          (list "ok" "OK" "a1" "--1" "..." ".." "." "" "/" "@" "i" "e" "e3"
                "+." "-." "_x" ":kw" "a@b" "%x" "^x" "~x" "&x" "*x*" "<=>"
                "set!" "null?" "list->vector" ".foo" "a.b" "0x10" "12abc"
                "#x10" "a b" "a\tb" "a\nb" "a(b" "a\"b"
                "\x4e2d;\x6587;" "\x1F600;")))
    (let number ((xs (append matrix compound others)) (k 0) (acc '()))
      (if (null? xs)
          (reverse acc)
          (number (cdr xs) (+ k 1)
                  (cons (list (string-append "sym-" (number->string k))
                              (car xs))
                        acc))))))

;; ---- extra write vectors the amendment calls for --------------------
;; NaN is not one bit pattern: a quiet NaN with a payload, and a
;; negative one, must survive as BITS.  These are built from raw IEEE
;; bytes rather than from any decimal text, which is the whole point of
;; the #f8 form.
(define (bytes->double b0 b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 b6 b7)
  (let ((bv (make-bytevector 8)))
    (bytevector-u8-set! bv 0 b0) (bytevector-u8-set! bv 1 b1)
    (bytevector-u8-set! bv 2 b2) (bytevector-u8-set! bv 3 b3)
    (bytevector-u8-set! bv 4 b4) (bytevector-u8-set! bv 5 b5)
    (bytevector-u8-set! bv 6 b6) (bytevector-u8-set! bv 7 b7)
    (bytevector-ieee-double-ref bv 0 (endianness little))))

(define extra-write-cases
  (list
   (list "flonum-pos-zero" "flonum" 0.0)
   (list "flonum-min-subnormal" "flonum" (bytes->double 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 0))
   (list "flonum-min-normal" "flonum" (bytes->double 0 0 0 0 0 0 16 0))
   (list "flonum-max" "flonum" (bytes->double 255 255 255 255 255 255 239 127))
   (list "flonum-nan-payload" "flonum" (bytes->double 1 0 0 0 0 0 248 127))
   (list "flonum-nan-negative" "flonum" (bytes->double 0 0 0 0 0 0 248 255))
   (list "deep-64-atom" "depth"
         (let loop ((k 0) (x 1)) (if (= k 64) x (loop (+ k 1) (list x)))))
   (list "string-64k" "string" (make-string 65600 #\a))
   (list "symbol-token-cap" "symbol"
         (string->symbol (make-string 65536 #\a)))
   (list "list-flat-long" "list"
         (let loop ((k 0) (acc (quote ()))) (if (= k 3000) acc (loop (+ k 1) (cons k acc)))))
   (list "bytevector-64k" "bytevector" (make-bytevector 70000 7))))

;; ---- anchors: the value model as a matrix, not as a list ------------
;;
;; The golden groups above are FIXED-POINT checks -- parse the
;; authority's bytes, write them back, compare -- and a fixed point is
;; blind to a matching pair of mistakes on the two sides of it.  What a
;; wire string MEANS is asked here instead: each anchor carries a value
;; description that the downstream suites build natively, without
;; touching their own codec, plus the bytes the authority produces for
;; it.  Both directions are then asserted against those bytes.
;;
;; This started as a handful of anchors, each added after a specific
;; paired mutation was demonstrated to survive without it, and it grew
;; the way the symbol corpus once did: a reviewer names a survivor, one
;; more anchor appears, and what is still uncovered stays a question
;; about anyone's imagination.  The corpus was closed by making it a
;; cross product; so is this.  The matrix below is type x spelling
;; branch over the whole value model, the branch names are pinned
;; downstream, and "which paired mutation could still live" is answered
;; by the dimensions rather than by whoever reviews it next.
;;
;; SPEC LANGUAGE.  A prefix token program, whitespace separated, whose
;; every payload is a decimal integer -- so a spec can be read on any
;; host without a second layer of string escaping to get wrong, which
;; is the layer these very tests exist to check.  Text is carried as
;; UTF-8 BYTES and each host decodes them into its own string model.
;;
;;   T  F  NIL                 booleans, the empty list
;;   N <int>                   exact integer (any size)
;;   Q <int> <int>             exact ratio, numerator then denominator
;;   D <b0>..<b7>              flonum, from its 8 IEEE bytes little-endian
;;   S <n> <byte>*n            string, from n UTF-8 bytes
;;   Y <n> <byte>*n            symbol, likewise
;;   B <n> <byte>*n            bytevector
;;   L <n> <spec>*n            proper list of n elements
;;   P <n> <spec>*n <spec>     improper list: n elements, then the tail
;;   V <n> <spec>*n            vector
;;
;; The specs are DERIVED from the values below rather than hand-written
;; (a hand-typed byte list is its own source of silent error), and the
;; derivation is checked by re-reading each one before it is emitted.

;; The name of a nesting atom, computed FROM the atom.  Every consumer
;; computes THE SAME FUNCTION on the decoded value and compares by
;; equality, so the name is a fact about the datum and not a word
;; someone typed beside it.
;;
;; Re-derivation rather than a table of predicates, deliberately.  A
;; predicate table has to be mutually exclusive to mean anything, and
;; ours was not: `-0.0` answered both `negative-zero` and a generic
;; `flonum`, a string with a control character AND a multi-byte
;; sequence answered two labels, and the symbol arm answered
;; `punctuation-symbol` for `abc`.  A total function into one label
;; cannot have that problem, and the three copies are compared by
;; equality rather than by each side's opinion.
(define (nesting-label v)
  (define (string-label str)
    (let ((bv (string->utf8 str)))
      (let loop ((i 0) (control #f) (escape #f) (high #f))
        (if (= i (bytevector-length bv))
            (let ((n (+ (if control 1 0) (if escape 1 0) (if high 1 0))))
              (cond ((= n 0) "plain-string")
                    ((> n 1) "ambiguous-string")   ; refused below
                    (control "control-string")
                    (escape "escape-string")
                    (else "utf8-string")))
            (let ((b (bytevector-u8-ref bv i)))
              (loop (+ i 1)
                    (or control (< b 32))
                    (or escape (or (= b 34) (= b 92)))
                    (or high (>= b 128))))))))
  (cond
   ((eq? v #f) "false")
   ((eq? v #t) "true")
   ;; the two flonum labels are exclusive by construction, which the
   ;; predicate pair they replaced was not
   ((flonum? v) (if (eqv? v -0.0) "negative-zero" "flonum"))
   ((and (number? v) (exact? v) (integer? v))
    ;; The fixnum range is ASYMMETRIC -- i31 with a tag bit, so
    ;; -536870912 is a fixnum and 536870912 is not.  Testing |v| made
    ;; -536870912 a "bignum", which is the one integer the generator
    ;; itself names `fixnum-edge-negative` twenty lines away: every
    ;; bignum-* cell could have held a fixnum and stayed green.
    (if (or (> v 536870911) (< v -536870912)) "bignum" "small-integer"))
   ((and (number? v) (exact? v) (rational? v)) "ratio")
   ((bytevector? v)
    (string-append "bytevector-pad"
                   (number->string (mod (- 3 (mod (bytevector-length v) 3)) 3))))
   ((null? v) "empty-list")
   ((vector? v) "vector")
   ((pair? v)
    ;; the tail loop's null arm and its dotted arm are two branches, so
    ;; a proper list and a dotted pair are two atoms, not one
    (let walk ((x v))
      (cond ((null? (cdr x)) "proper-list")
            ((pair? (cdr x)) (walk (cdr x)))
            (else "dotted-pair"))))
   ((string? v) (string-label v))
   ((symbol? v)
    ;; `abc` used to answer "punctuation-symbol" because this arm was
    ;; unconditional, so the label claimed a property the value did not
    ;; have and both consumers disagreed with it
    (let ((str (symbol->string v)))
      (let loop ((i 0))
        (cond ((= i (string-length str)) "plain-symbol")
              ((memv (char->integer (string-ref str i))
                     '(42 43 45 60 61 62 63 33))
               "punctuation-symbol")
              (else (loop (+ i 1)))))))
   (else "no-label")))

;; An atom whose label is ambiguous or absent must not enter the product:
;; the label would then depend on how the classifier happens to break a
;; tie, and a later edit could move a value across that tie invisibly.
(define (checked-nesting-label v)
  (let ((label (nesting-label v)))
    (when (member label '("ambiguous-string" "no-label"))
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
             (format "this nesting atom has no single label (~a): ~s"
                     label v)))
    label))

(define (double->bytes x)
  (let ((bv (make-bytevector 8)))
    (bytevector-ieee-double-set! bv 0 x (endianness little))
    bv))

;; Flonums are compared as BITS: eqv? already separates -0.0 from 0.0 and
;; joins NaNs, but this file records NaN payloads, and "some NaN" is not
;; what an anchor is for.
(define (same-datum? a b)
  (cond ((and (flonum? a) (flonum? b))
         (bytevector=? (double->bytes a) (double->bytes b)))
        ((and (pair? a) (pair? b))
         (and (same-datum? (car a) (car b)) (same-datum? (cdr a) (cdr b))))
        ((and (vector? a) (vector? b))
         (and (= (vector-length a) (vector-length b))
              (let loop ((i 0))
                (or (= i (vector-length a))
                    (and (same-datum? (vector-ref a i) (vector-ref b i))
                         (loop (+ i 1)))))))
        ((and (string? a) (string? b)) (string=? a b))
        ((and (bytevector? a) (bytevector? b)) (bytevector=? a b))
        ((and (number? a) (number? b))
         (and (eq? (exact? a) (exact? b)) (= a b)))
        (else (eqv? a b))))

(define (bytes-spec bv)
  (let loop ((i 0) (acc '()))
    (if (= i (bytevector-length bv))
        (apply string-append (reverse acc))
        (loop (+ i 1)
              (cons (string-append " " (number->string (bytevector-u8-ref bv i)))
                    acc)))))

(define (value->spec v)
  (cond
   ((eq? v #t) "T")
   ((eq? v #f) "F")
   ((null? v) "NIL")
   ((and (number? v) (exact? v) (integer? v))
    (string-append "N " (number->string v)))
   ((and (number? v) (exact? v) (rational? v))
    (string-append "Q " (number->string (numerator v))
                   " " (number->string (denominator v))))
   ((flonum? v) (string-append "D" (bytes-spec (double->bytes v))))
   ((string? v)
    (let ((bv (string->utf8 v)))
      (string-append "S " (number->string (bytevector-length bv))
                     (bytes-spec bv))))
   ((symbol? v)
    (let ((bv (string->utf8 (symbol->string v))))
      (string-append "Y " (number->string (bytevector-length bv))
                     (bytes-spec bv))))
   ((bytevector? v)
    (string-append "B " (number->string (bytevector-length v))
                   (bytes-spec v)))
   ((vector? v)
    (let loop ((i 0) (acc (list (string-append "V " (number->string (vector-length v))))))
      (if (= i (vector-length v))
          (apply string-append (reverse acc))
          (loop (+ i 1) (cons (string-append " " (value->spec (vector-ref v i))) acc)))))
   ((pair? v)
    (let count ((x v) (n 0) (elems '()))
      (if (pair? x)
          (count (cdr x) (+ n 1) (cons (car x) elems))
          (let* ((elems (reverse elems))
                 (head (if (null? x)
                           (string-append "L " (number->string n))
                           (string-append "P " (number->string n))))
                 (body (apply string-append
                              (map (lambda (e) (string-append " " (value->spec e))) elems))))
            (string-append head body
                           (if (null? x) "" (string-append " " (value->spec x))))))))
   (else (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                (format "no spec form for ~s -- the anchor matrix cannot \
                         describe a value the spec language has no tag for" v)))))

;; The inverse, used only to check the derivation above before anything
;; is written.  The downstream suites implement this same reading in
;; their own languages; that they agree with this one is exactly what
;; the wire bytes beside each spec then confirm.
(define (spec-tokens s)
  (let loop ((i 0) (start 0) (acc '()))
    (cond ((= i (string-length s))
           (reverse (if (> i start) (cons (substring s start i) acc) acc)))
          ((char=? (string-ref s i) #\space)
           (loop (+ i 1) (+ i 1)
                 (if (> i start) (cons (substring s start i) acc) acc)))
          (else (loop (+ i 1) start acc)))))

(define (spec-read toks)
  ;; -> (values value remaining-tokens); raises if the program is
  ;; malformed.  THIS IS THE THIRD READER of the spec language -- the
  ;; two suites have their own -- and until this round it was the only
  ;; one not held to the shared list of programs that must be refused.
  ;; That is how `N Z` came to denote 42 in the consumers (their digit
  ;; folding subtracted #\0 from any character) while this one answered
  ;; #f: three readers, three different wrong answers, and nothing
  ;; comparing them.  spec-readers-agree! below runs the list here.
  (define (bad why) (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors (string-append "spec: " why)))
  ;; The bytes of an S or Y payload must BE a string.  They are not
  ;; automatically: `S 1 255` is a one-byte #xff, which this host turns
  ;; into U+FFFD, the Goeteia reader keeps as a byte string, and the JS
  ;; reader refuses outright -- one program, three values.  The spec
  ;; language describes data that crosses this wire, and the wire has no
  ;; string that is not well-formed UTF-8, so it is refused everywhere.
  (define (spec-utf8 bv)
    (let ((str (guard (e (#t #f)) (utf8->string bv))))
      (unless (and str (bytevector=? (string->utf8 str) bv))
        (bad "payload is not well-formed UTF-8"))
      str))
  (define (int t)
    ;; DIGITS ONLY, and at least one.  string->number would take "1/2",
    ;; "#xff", "1e3" and "+5"; the spec language has exactly one numeral
    ;; form and a reader that accepts more is a reader that disagrees
    ;; with the other two.
    (let* ((m (string-length t))
           (neg (and (> m 0) (char=? (string-ref t 0) #\-)))
           (start (if neg 1 0)))
      (when (= start m) (bad (string-append "not a numeral: " t)))
      (let loop ((i start) (acc 0))
        (cond ((= i m) (if neg (- 0 acc) acc))
              ((and (char<=? #\0 (string-ref t i))
                    (char<=? (string-ref t i) #\9))
               (loop (+ i 1)
                     (+ (* acc 10) (- (char->integer (string-ref t i)) 48))))
              (else (bad (string-append "not a numeral: " t)))))))
  (define (count t)
    (let ((n (int t)))
      (when (< n 0) (bad "negative count"))
      n))
  (when (null? toks) (bad "ends where a datum was due"))
  (let ((tag (car toks)) (r (cdr toks)))
    (define (arg k) (if (null? r) (bad "ends where an argument was due") (k)))
    (define (take-bytes n rest)
      (let loop ((k 0) (rest rest) (acc '()))
        (cond ((= k n) (values (u8-list->bytevector (reverse acc)) rest))
              ((null? rest) (bad "ends inside a payload"))
              (else
               (let ((b (int (car rest))))
                 (unless (and (>= b 0) (< b 256)) (bad "byte out of range"))
                 (loop (+ k 1) (cdr rest) (cons b acc)))))))
    (define (take-values n rest)
      (let loop ((k 0) (rest rest) (acc '()))
        (if (= k n)
            (values (reverse acc) rest)
            (let-values (((v rest2) (spec-read rest)))
              (loop (+ k 1) rest2 (cons v acc))))))
    (cond
     ((string=? tag "T") (values #t r))
     ((string=? tag "F") (values #f r))
     ((string=? tag "NIL") (values '() r))
     ((string=? tag "N") (arg (lambda () (values (int (car r)) (cdr r)))))
     ((string=? tag "Q")
      (arg (lambda ()
             (when (null? (cdr r)) (bad "ends where a denominator was due"))
             (let ((d (int (cadr r))))
               (when (= d 0) (bad "zero denominator"))
               (let ((q (/ (int (car r)) d)))
                 ;; ...and Q must denote a RATIO.  `Q 4 2` is 2 and
                 ;; `Q 0 7` is 0, which `N 2` and `N 0` already spell --
                 ;; the same aliasing as P, one tag over: a tag that can
                 ;; produce a datum outside the kind it names has
                 ;; stopped naming that kind.  (A ratio that merely
                 ;; needs reducing, like `Q 2 4`, is still a ratio and
                 ;; is fine -- the corpus uses it on purpose.)
                 (when (integer? q)
                   (bad "ratio that is a whole number"))
                 (values q (cddr r)))))))
     ((string=? tag "D")
      (let-values (((bv rest) (take-bytes 8 r)))
        (values (bytevector-ieee-double-ref bv 0 (endianness little)) rest)))
     ((string=? tag "S")
      (arg (lambda ()
             (let-values (((bv rest) (take-bytes (count (car r)) (cdr r))))
               (values (spec-utf8 bv) rest)))))
     ((string=? tag "Y")
      (arg (lambda ()
             (let-values (((bv rest) (take-bytes (count (car r)) (cdr r))))
               (values (string->symbol (spec-utf8 bv)) rest)))))
     ((string=? tag "B")
      (arg (lambda () (take-bytes (count (car r)) (cdr r)))))
     ((string=? tag "L")
      (arg (lambda ()
             (let-values (((vs rest) (take-values (count (car r)) (cdr r))))
               (values vs rest)))))
     ((string=? tag "P")
      ;; at least one element: `P 0 X` denotes X, a second spelling of a
      ;; datum that already has one.  value->spec can only produce P
      ;; from a pair, so this rule costs nothing here -- but a rule the
      ;; consumers enforce and this reader does not is three readers
      ;; disagreeing again.
      (arg (lambda ()
             (let ((n (count (car r))))
               (when (= n 0) (bad "improper list with no elements"))
               (let-values (((vs rest) (take-values n (cdr r))))
                 (let-values (((tail rest2) (spec-read rest)))
                   ;; ...and the tail must be an ATOM -- neither the
                   ;; empty list nor a pair.  This is the same fact
                   ;; about the format that degenerate-reason uses: a
                   ;; tail that is a pair or () merges into the
                   ;; enclosing list, so `P 1 N 1 NIL` is `(1)` and
                   ;; `P 1 N 1 L 1 N 2` is `(1 2)` and
                   ;; `P 1 N 1 P 1 N 2 N 3` is `(1 2 . 3)`.  Each is a
                   ;; second spelling of a datum that already has one.
                   ;;
                   ;; The first fix here refused only the empty list --
                   ;; one level of the same aliasing -- and a nested `P`
                   ;; tail walked straight through it.  One rule, stated
                   ;; once: the tail is not a list.
                   (when (or (null? tail) (pair? tail))
                     (bad "improper list whose tail is itself a list"))
                   (values (let build ((xs vs))
                             (if (null? xs) tail (cons (car xs) (build (cdr xs)))))
                           rest2)))))))
     ((string=? tag "V")
      (arg (lambda ()
             (let-values (((vs rest) (take-values (count (car r)) (cdr r))))
               (values (list->vector vs) rest)))))
     (else (bad (string-append "unknown tag " tag))))))

(define (spec->value s)
  (let-values (((v rest) (spec-read (spec-tokens s))))
    (unless (null? rest)
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
             (string-append "trailing tokens in spec: " s)))
    v))

;; THE SHARED LIST.  Every reader of this language must refuse exactly
;; these, or the language means different things in different places and
;; a spec that reads one way here reads another way downstream.  The two
;; suites carry the same list; this one is run at generation time so the
;; generator cannot drift away from the readers it writes for.
(define malformed-specs
  '(""                    ; nothing at all
    "N"                   ; a tag with its argument missing
    "N 1 N 2"             ; tokens left over
    "N Z"                 ; not a numeral -- folding any character as a
                          ; digit made this 42 in both consumers
    "N -"                 ; a sign and no digits
    "N 1.5"               ; one numeral form only
    "N +5"
    "Q 1 0"               ; zero denominator is not a datum
    "Q 1"                 ; denominator missing
    "Z"                   ; unknown tag
    "L 1 Z"               ; unknown tag NESTED -- the false green
    "L 1 Z N 1"
    "V 1 Z"
    "P 1 N 1 Z"           ; unknown tag in the tail
    "L 3 N 1 N 2"         ; count runs past the end
    "L -1"                ; a negative count
    "S 4 65 66"           ; byte count runs past the end
    "S -1"
    "B 1 256"             ; not a byte
    "B 1 -1"
    "D 0 0 0"             ; short flonum
    "P 0 N 1"             ; a second spelling of its own tail
    ;; every spelling of a tail that is itself a list: each aliases a
    ;; datum that already has a spelling, and refusing only the first
    ;; left the other two walking through
    "P 1 N 1 NIL"         ; = (1),       which L 1 N 1 spells
    "P 1 N 1 L 1 N 2"     ; = (1 2),     which L 2 N 1 N 2 spells
    "P 1 N 1 P 1 N 2 N 3" ; = (1 2 . 3), which P 2 N 1 N 2 N 3 spells
    ;; the same shape one tag over: a Q that is not a ratio
    "Q 4 2"               ; = 2, which N 2 spells
    "Q 0 7"               ; = 0, which N 0 spells
    ;; not a string: a lone #xff, a truncated sequence, an overlong
    ;; form and a surrogate half.  Each of these read as three
    ;; different values in the three readers (U+FFFD here, a byte
    ;; string in Goeteia, a refusal in JS) until they were refused.
    "S 1 255"
    "Y 1 255"
    "S 2 226 152"
    "S 2 192 175"
    "S 3 237 160 128"))

;; The (program . why) PAIR is what the consumers pin -- not the size,
;; which let a program be swapped for an equivalent one, and not the
;; `why` alone, which let a program be swapped while its claim stayed.
;; All 23 pairs are stated in both, the same treatment the branch matrix
;; gets: a new claimed reader path should be a decision in both
;; consumers rather than a number that drifts.
;;
;; NOT alias-free, on purpose.  `Q 1 -2` and `Q -1 2` denote one value
;; and record one wire; `Q 2 4` needs reducing.  That is what this half
;; is FOR: each spelling reaches the reader's normalisation by a
;; different route (the sign arriving on the denominator is not the same
;; code path as the sign arriving on the numerator), so a reader that
;; handled one and not the other is caught.  Aliasing is a defect in a
;; tag that claims a KIND -- `P` naming an improper list, `Q` naming a
;; ratio -- and a feature in a corpus that exists to exercise
;; normalisation.
;;
;; Accepting a program is not the same as reading it RIGHT.  These were
;; checked only for "does not raise", so a reader that took a ratio as
;; n/|d| made `Q 1 -2` denote +1/2 and stayed on the list.  Each program
;; is therefore emitted with the wire the AUTHORITY produces for the
;; value it denotes, and every reader is held to that: read the program,
;; write the value, compare bytes.
(define wellformed-specs
  ;; (program . why).  The PAIR is what the consumers pin -- see above.
  ;; Pinning only the size and the malformed regressions left this half
  ;; substitutable: swapping `Q 1 -2` for `N -0` kept the count, the
  ;; textual uniqueness and every value/wire comparison green while the
  ;; denominator-sign path stopped being exercised at all.  A program
  ;; can be replaced by an equivalent one; a claimed reader path cannot
  ;; go missing without a name disappearing.  Same shape as the holes:
  ;; the reason is data.
  '(("N 42"        . "a plain numeral")
    ("N -42"       . "a negative numeral")
    ("N 0"         . "zero")
    ("T"           . "true")
    ("F"           . "false -- and the value #f is not the failure #f")
    ("NIL"         . "the empty list")
    ("Q 355 113"   . "a ratio already in lowest terms")
    ("Q 1 -2"      . "the sign arriving on the DENOMINATOR")
    ("Q -1 2"      . "the sign arriving on the NUMERATOR")
    ("Q 2 4"       . "a ratio that needs reducing")
    ("L 2 N 1 S 1 65" . "a list of mixed kinds")
    ("L 0"         . "the counted-list path at length zero")
    ("P 1 N 1 N 2" . "an improper list")
    ("V 2 T NIL"   . "a vector of mixed kinds")
    ("V 0"         . "the empty vector, which is not the empty list")
    ("B 2 1 2"     . "a bytevector")
    ("B 0"         . "the empty bytevector")
    ("D 0 0 0 0 0 0 248 63" . "a flonum from its bits")
    ("D 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 128"  . "a NEGATIVE ZERO, whose sign no decimal carries")
    ("S 0"         . "the empty string")
    ("Y 2 111 107" . "a symbol")
    ("S 4 240 159 152 128" . "a four-byte UTF-8 sequence")
    ("S 3 97 10 98" . "a control character inside a string")))

(define (wellformed-program e) (car e))
(define (wellformed-why e) (cdr e))

(define (spec-refused? s)
  (guard (e (#t #t)) (spec->value s) #f))

;; The `whole-alphabet` bytevector has to actually use the whole
;; alphabet.  A branch name that describes a property of its value is
;; the same kind of claim as any other here, and this one was false for
;; a long time without anything noticing.
(define (whole-alphabet-is-whole!)
  (let* ((v (let find ((cs (anchor-cases)))
              (cond ((null? cs)
                     (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors "no whole-alphabet case"))
                    ((string=? (cadr (car cs)) "whole-alphabet")
                     (caddr (car cs)))
                    (else (find (cdr cs))))))
         (text (base64-encode v)))
    ;; EQUALITY, not "every character occurs somewhere".  The comment
    ;; beside the value claims 64 characters, in alphabet order, once
    ;; each, without padding; an occurrence test is weaker than that on
    ;; every count -- a permutation passes it, so does the alphabet
    ;; followed by more groups.  An assertion weaker than the sentence
    ;; next to it is the shape this whole batch has been about.
    (unless (string=? text b64-alphabet)
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
             (string-append "the whole-alphabet bytevector encodes to\n  "
                            text "\nand not to the alphabet itself\n  "
                            b64-alphabet "\n-- its name is false")))))

(define b64-alphabet
  "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/")

(define (spec-readers-agree!)
(whole-alphabet-is-whole!)
  (for-each
   (lambda (bad)
     (unless (spec-refused? bad)
       (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
              (string-append "this reader accepts a spec the consumers "
                             "refuse, so the spec language means two "
                             "things: " (if (string=? bad "") "<empty>" bad)))))
   malformed-specs)
  ;; ...and the list is only evidence if the reader accepts anything at
  ;; all: a reader that refused everything would satisfy every line above
  (for-each
   (lambda (e)
     (when (spec-refused? (wellformed-program e))
       (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
              (string-append "this reader refuses a well-formed spec: "
                             (wellformed-program e)))))
   wellformed-specs))

;; (type branch value [wire-override])
;;
;; A wire-override is a spelling the authority ACCEPTS but does not
;; itself emit -- the escaped and raw spellings of a control character
;; are the same value written two ways, and only one of them is what
;; the writer produces.  Which is which is not declared here: the
;; generator writes the value, compares, and records the direction it
;; measured.
(define (fl-bytes b0 b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 b6 b7)
  (bytes->double b0 b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 b6 b7))

;; A FUNCTION, not a value.  The escaped-spelling cells build their wire
;; override by asking the authority to write the value -- a measurement
;; -- and as a top-level definition that ran during load, before any of
;; the guards below had decided whether to trust it.  The comment on
;; anchor-records claimed every measurement was delayed; it was true of
;; anchor-record and false of this.
(define (anchor-cases)
  (append
   (list
    (list "boolean" "true" #t)
    (list "boolean" "false" #f)
    (list "null" "empty-list" '())
    (list "integer" "zero" 0)
    (list "integer" "positive" 42)
    (list "integer" "negative" -42)
    (list "integer" "bignum-positive" (expt 2 90))
    (list "integer" "bignum-negative" (- (expt 2 90)))
    (list "integer" "fixnum-edge-positive" 536870911)
    (list "integer" "fixnum-edge-negative" -536870912)
    (list "integer" "fixnum-edge-past" 536870912)
    (list "ratio" "positive" 355/113)
    (list "ratio" "negative" -7/2)
    (list "flonum" "half" 1.5)
    (list "flonum" "integral" 1.0)
    (list "flonum" "repeating" 0.1)
    (list "flonum" "negative" -2.75)
    (list "flonum" "positive-zero" 0.0)
    (list "flonum" "negative-zero" -0.0)
    (list "flonum" "positive-infinity" (fl-bytes 0 0 0 0 0 0 240 127))
    (list "flonum" "negative-infinity" (fl-bytes 0 0 0 0 0 0 240 255))
    (list "flonum" "nan-quiet" (fl-bytes 0 0 0 0 0 0 248 127))
    (list "flonum" "nan-payload" (fl-bytes 1 0 0 0 0 0 248 127))
    (list "flonum" "nan-negative" (fl-bytes 0 0 0 0 0 0 248 255))
    (list "flonum" "min-subnormal" (fl-bytes 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 0))
    (list "flonum" "min-normal" (fl-bytes 0 0 0 0 0 0 16 0))
    (list "flonum" "max-finite" (fl-bytes 255 255 255 255 255 255 239 127))
    (list "string" "empty" "")
    (list "string" "ascii" "abc")
    (list "string" "quote" "a\"b")
    (list "string" "backslash" "a\\b")
    ;; the UTF-8 sequence lengths are a dimension of their own, and it
    ;; had 1, 3 and 4 in it but not 2 -- a dimension present in three of
    ;; its four values is exactly the shape the symbol corpus was caught
    ;; by five separate times
    (list "string" "latin1" "\xe9;")            ; 2 bytes: c3 a9
    (list "string" "cjk" "\x4e2d;\x6587;")      ; 3 bytes each
    (list "string" "astral" "\x1F600;")         ; 4 bytes
    (list "symbol" "plain" 'ok)
    (list "symbol" "hyphenated" 'get-user)
    (list "symbol" "punctuation" (string->symbol "a*-+<=>?!b"))
    (list "symbol" "case-lower" (string->symbol "abc"))
    (list "symbol" "case-upper" (string->symbol "ABC"))
    (list "symbol" "case-mixed" (string->symbol "aBc"))
    ;; There is deliberately no non-ASCII symbol branch: the authority
    ;; refuses to write one, so no wire exists to anchor it to.  Which
    ;; names it refuses is not this matrix's question -- write_reject
    ;; above is a cross product over exactly that surface.
    (list "bytevector" "empty" (bytevector))
    (list "bytevector" "one-byte" (bytevector 255))
    (list "bytevector" "two-bytes" (bytevector 1 2))
    (list "bytevector" "three-bytes" (bytevector 1 2 3))
    (list "bytevector" "zero-byte" (bytevector 0))
    (list "bytevector" "whole-alphabet"
          ;; 48 bytes -> 64 base64 characters with no padding.  BUILT so
          ;; the 64 six-bit groups are 0..63 in order, which is exactly
          ;; the alphabet once each; the previous value (k*21 mod 256)
          ;; produced only 38 distinct characters, so the branch name
          ;; was false and nothing here would have said so.  Constructed
          ;; rather than tabulated, and checked below, so the name is a
          ;; claim rather than a hope.
          (let ((bv (make-bytevector 48 0)))
            (let pack ((g 0) (acc 0) (bits 0) (i 0))
              (cond ((= g 64) bv)
                    ((>= (+ bits 6) 8)
                     (let ((n (+ (* acc 64) g)))
                       (bytevector-u8-set!
                        bv i (mod (div n (expt 2 (- (+ bits 6) 8))) 256))
                       (pack (+ g 1) (mod n (expt 2 (- (+ bits 6) 8)))
                             (- (+ bits 6) 8) (+ i 1))))
                    (else (pack (+ g 1) (+ (* acc 64) g) (+ bits 6) i))))))
    (list "vector" "empty" (vector))
    (list "vector" "single" (vector 1))
    (list "vector" "nested" (vector 1 (vector 2) 3))
    (list "vector" "mixed" (vector 'a "b" #t 1.5))
    (list "list" "single" (list 1))
    (list "list" "proper" (list 1 2 3))
    (list "list" "dotted-pair" (cons 1 2))
    (list "list" "dotted-tail" (cons 1 (cons 2 3)))
    (list "list" "nested" (list 1 (list 2 (list 3 4)) 5))
    (list "list" "alist" (list (cons 'a 1) (cons 'b 2)))
    (list "list" "mixed" (list 'get-user 42 "x" #t))
    (list "list" "containing-empty" (list '() (vector))))
   ;; POSITION is a dimension, and it was the corner of the product that
   ;; was filled in.  Every compound above held integers, symbols,
   ;; strings and booleans; no anchor put a bytevector, a ratio, a
   ;; bignum, a negative zero or a control character anywhere but at top
   ;; level.  The emitter takes a depth argument, so it can be wrong for
   ;; one of those only when nested, and nothing here would have said so.
   ;;
   ;; WHICH sub-dimensions get crossed with position is not "all of
   ;; them".  The rule, and it was arrived at the hard way:
   ;;
   ;;   a sub-dimension belongs in this product exactly when it is a
   ;;   real BRANCH in the code -- something a mutation conditioned on
   ;;   depth could target.
   ;;
   ;; The negative examples are what make that rule usable, so they are
   ;; recorded here rather than left to be rediscovered:
   ;;   * padding class IS a branch (in base64-encode), and a paired
   ;;     mutation reversing two-byte bytevectors only when nested lived
   ;;     through every suite until all three classes appeared nested;
   ;;   * padded versus unpadded base64 SPELLING is not: `=` is skipped
   ;;     and never counted, so "AQI" and "AQI=" traverse identical code
   ;;     and no mutation can separate them -- an anchor for it would be
   ;;     a check that cannot fail;
   ;;   * a flonum's class (inf / nan / zero) is not: every double takes
   ;;     one path out of the writer.
   ;;
   ;; The empty list IS an atom here.  It was left out on the reasoning
   ;; that consing it as a tail gives a proper list -- true, but that
   ;; argues against one CELL, not against the whole row, and leaving
   ;; the row out made a mutation on the later-element call site
   ;; invisible to everything.  That cell is now a recorded hole (see
   ;; degenerate-reason), which is the honest shape: the row exists, and
   ;; the one position the format cannot express is written down with
   ;; its reason.  (The claim that the duplicate check would have caught
   ;; the cell was also false -- no `(head)` anchor exists for it to
   ;; collide with.)
   ;;
   ;; The label is DERIVED from the value, never declared beside it.  A
   ;; declared one is a claim, and every claim here was found to be
   ;; forgeable: swapping the bignum for 255 left the `bignum-*` branches
   ;; covering no bignum, and giving `bytevector-pad1` a four-byte value
   ;; silently moved it into the `==` class -- which is to say it undid
   ;; the fix above with every check still green.  Derived, the name
   ;; cannot disagree with the value it names.
   (let loop ((as (list (bytes->double 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 128)   ; negative zero
                        -7/2
                        (expt 2 90)
                        (bytevector 255)                    ; len mod 3 = 1
                        (bytevector 1 2)                    ; len mod 3 = 2
                        (bytevector 1 2 3)                  ; len mod 3 = 0
                        (string #\a #\newline #\b)          ; control character
                        "a\"b\\c"                            ; the escaped pair
                        "\xe9;\x4e2d;\x1F600;"                 ; UTF-8 2, 3 and 4
                        (string->symbol "a*-+<=>?!b")
                        ;; both booleans: they are two cond arms in the
                        ;; writer, and with only #f here a mutation that
                        ;; wrote a #t tail as #f survived everything
                        #f
                        #t
                        ;; and the COMPOUNDS.  `()`, a vector, a proper
                        ;; list and a dotted pair are four separate arms
                        ;; of the writer, and none of them had ever been
                        ;; nested: a mutation writing `()` as `#f` in
                        ;; the later-element call site alone survived
                        ;; every suite.  They were left out because the
                        ;; product-fullness check only forms its rows
                        ;; from cells that carry an atom column, so a
                        ;; type with no cells at all is invisible to it
                        ;; -- absence cannot be spotted by a check that
                        ;; enumerates what is present.
                        '()
                        (vector 1)
                        (list 1 2)
                        (cons 1 2)))
              (acc '()))
     (if (null? as)
         (reverse acc)
         (let* ((v (car as))
                (label (checked-nesting-label v)))
           (loop (cdr as)
                 (append
                  (reverse
                   (list
                    ;; FIRST element and a LATER element are two call
                    ;; sites in the writer (emit (car x) ...) and the
                    ;; tail loop's (emit (car x) ...) -- so they are two
                    ;; branches, and every cell here used to place its
                    ;; subject second.  A mutation in the first-element
                    ;; site alone stayed green.
                    ;;
                    ;; A vector deliberately gets no such pair: its
                    ;; elements all go through ONE call, with only the
                    ;; separating space conditional, so there is no
                    ;; branch for a mutation to sit in and the cell
                    ;; could not fail.
                    (list "nesting" (string-append label "-head-of-list")
                          (list v 'last) #f label "head-of-list")
                    (list "nesting" (string-append label "-in-list")
                          (list 'head v 'last) #f label "in-list")
                    (list "nesting" (string-append label "-in-vector")
                          (vector 'head v 'last) #f label "in-vector")
                    (list "nesting" (string-append label "-as-tail")
                          (cons 'head v) #f label "as-tail")
                    ;; depth two: every other cell is depth one, so a
                    ;; writer that only goes wrong below the first level
                    ;; had no cell to fail in
                    (list "nesting" (string-append label "-deep")
                          (list 'head (list 'inner v 'inner2) 'last)
                          #f label "deep")))
                  acc)))))
   ;; ACCEPTED SPELLING x POSITION.  The control characters below appear
   ;; in both spellings, but only at top level, so the escaped spelling
   ;; had no nested cell: a mutation that passes depth into parse-string
   ;; and reads `\n` wrongly only when depth > 0 stayed green.  The
   ;; escape table is one region of code, so one control character at
   ;; every position exercises it -- crossing all three would add cells
   ;; that no mutation separates.
   ;;
   ;; The override is not typed out.  It is the authority's own wire for
   ;; the same value with the raw byte rewritten as its escape, so the
   ;; only hand-made part is the substitution rule, and the generator
   ;; then measures that the authority accepts the result.
   (let* ((esc (lambda (wire)
                 (let loop ((i 0) (acc '()))
                   (if (= i (string-length wire))
                       (list->string (reverse acc))
                       (let ((c (string-ref wire i)))
                         (loop (+ i 1)
                               (if (char=? c #\newline)
                                   (cons #\n (cons #\\ acc))
                                   (cons c acc))))))))
          (v "a\nb")
          (cell (lambda (position build)
                  (let ((datum (build v)))
                    (list "nesting"
                          (string-append "control-string-" position "-escaped")
                          datum
                          (esc (sexpr->string-extended datum))
                          "control-string" position)))))
     ;; ALL FIVE positions.  When head-of-list was added to the product
     ;; above it was not added here, so `direction=read` existed at four
     ;; positions and not the fifth -- the same one-edge-of-the-product
     ;; shape this whole section exists to prevent, introduced by the
     ;; edit that fixed the previous instance of it.  A pair sweep found
     ;; it; nothing else would have.
     (list (cell "head-of-list" (lambda (x) (list x 'last)))
           (cell "in-list" (lambda (x) (list 'head x 'last)))
           (cell "in-vector" (lambda (x) (vector 'head x 'last)))
           (cell "as-tail" (lambda (x) (cons 'head x)))
           (cell "deep" (lambda (x) (list 'head (list 'inner x 'inner2) 'last)))))
   ;; The control characters, each as the value and as BOTH spellings of
   ;; it.  A round trip is satisfied by either spelling alone; that a
   ;; particular byte in the wire denotes a particular byte in the string
   ;; is what these ask.  The escape-form entries carry a wire override,
   ;; and the generator records which direction the authority supports.
   (let loop ((cs (list (list "newline" 10 "\\n")
                        (list "tab" 9 "\\t")
                        (list "return" 13 "\\r")))
              (acc '()))
     (if (null? cs)
         (reverse acc)
         (let* ((c (car cs))
                (label (car c))
                (byte (cadr c))
                (escape (caddr c))
                (v (string-append "a" (string (integer->char byte)) "b")))
           (loop (cdr cs)
                 (cons (list "string" (string-append "control-" label "-escaped") v
                             (string-append "\"a" escape "b\""))
                       (cons (list "string" (string-append "control-" label "-raw") v
                                   (string-append "\"a" (string (integer->char byte)) "b\""))
                             acc))))))))

;; Every anchor, measured.  The generator refuses to emit one it cannot
;; verify: the spec must denote the value, and the authority must read
;; the recorded wire back to exactly that value.
;; The atom that a nesting cell claims to be about, dug out of the
;; compound by the position the cell names.  These two must agree with
;; the value, or the branch name is the only thing asserting a
;; bytevector is involved and a name is not a check.
(define (atom-at position v)
  (cond ((string=? position "head-of-list") (car v))
        ((string=? position "in-list") (cadr v))
        ((string=? position "in-vector") (vector-ref v 1))
        ((string=? position "as-tail") (cdr v))
        ((string=? position "deep") (cadr (cadr v)))
        (else (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                     (string-append "no extractor for position " position)))))

(define (type-name-of v)
  (cond ((boolean? v) "boolean")
        ((null? v) "null")
        ((and (number? v) (exact? v) (integer? v)) "integer")
        ((and (number? v) (exact? v) (rational? v)) "ratio")
        ((flonum? v) "flonum")
        ((string? v) "string")
        ((symbol? v) "symbol")
        ((bytevector? v) "bytevector")
        ((vector? v) "vector")
        ((pair? v) "list")
        (else "?")))

(define (anchor-record c)
  ;; -> (type branch spec wire direction atom position)
  (let* ((type (car c))
         (branch (cadr c))
         (v (caddr c))
         (override (and (pair? (cdddr c)) (cadddr c)))
         (atom (and (> (length c) 4) (list-ref c 4)))
         (position (and (> (length c) 5) (list-ref c 5)))
         (spec (value->spec v))
         (where (string-append type "/" branch)))
    (unless (same-datum? (spec->value spec) v)
      (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
             (string-append "the derived spec for " where
                            " does not read back as the value it describes: "
                            spec)))
    ;; The claim the cell makes about itself, checked against the value
    ;; it was built from.  Swapping the bytevector atom for the integer
    ;; 255 used to leave every "bytevector-*" branch holding an integer
    ;; with nothing red anywhere.
    ;; Derived from the value at that position by the SAME function that
    ;; named the cell, so the two cannot disagree; and the branch name
    ;; must be exactly label-position, so a fixture that renamed the
    ;; position while keeping the atom (recording a "deep" cell as
    ;; "in-list", which finds the intermediate list and passes a loose
    ;; type test) is a mismatch here.
    ;; The `type` column of every OTHER anchor, checked the same way.
    ;; It was validated only for nesting cells, which left type-name-of
    ;; below with no caller at all -- a dead classifier beside a column
    ;; nothing here verified.  The consumers do check it against the
    ;; decoded value, so this closes the generation end of the same
    ;; claim rather than adding a new one.
    (unless position
      (let ((found (type-name-of v)))
        (unless (string=? found type)
          (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                 (string-append where " is filed under " type
                                ", but its value is a " found)))))
    ;; ...and every anchor carries the fine label too, not only the
    ;; nesting cells.  Without it a top-level branch name was checked
    ;; against nothing finer than its type: swapping the values and
    ;; wires of `boolean/true` and `boolean/false` left both names false
    ;; with everything green.  What this does NOT discriminate is a
    ;; branch pair that shares a label -- `integer/positive` and
    ;; `integer/negative` are both `small-integer` -- so those names
    ;; remain conventions rather than checked claims.
    (when position
      (let ((found (nesting-label (atom-at position v))))
        (unless (string=? found atom)
          (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                 (string-append where " says it puts a " atom " " position
                                ", but the value has a " found " there")))
        (unless (or (string=? branch (string-append atom "-" position))
                    (string=? branch
                              (string-append atom "-" position "-escaped")))
          (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                 (string-append where "'s name does not say what it is: "
                                "expected " atom "-" position
                                " (optionally + \"-escaped\")")))))
    (let* ((written (guard (e (#t #f)) (sexpr->string-extended v)))
           (wire (or override written)))
      (unless (string? wire)
        (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
               (string-append "the authority refuses to write " where
                              " and no wire was supplied for it")))
      (let ((back (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (string->sexpr-extended wire))))
        (unless (same-datum? back v)
          (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                 (string-append "the authority does not read " where
                                "'s wire back as the anchored value: " wire))))
      (list type branch spec wire
            (if (and (string? written) (string=? written wire)) "both" "read")
            (or atom (nesting-label v)) position))))

;; Computed inside main, not at load time: every other measurement in
;; this file happens after the guards below have run, and an anchor that
;; consulted the authority while the script was still deciding whether
;; to trust it would be the one measurement taken out of order.
;; A cell whose NAME is true but whose wire does not exercise the branch
;; the name refers to.  The format collapses `(a . ())` to `(a)` and
;; `(a . (b c))` to `(a b c)`, so an empty list or a proper list placed
;; "as a tail" produces a wire with no dot in it at all: the cell would
;; sit in the product claiming to exercise the writer's dotted arm while
;; going through the proper-list arm instead.  The label check passes
;; -- the tail really is an empty list -- so the discriminator has to be
;; the wire's own shape.
;;
;; Such cells are DROPPED and RECORDED, not quietly skipped: the fixture
;; carries them as holes with the reason, and the consumers require the
;; product to be full except exactly those.  A hole is then a fact about
;; the format that someone can read, rather than a cell nobody noticed
;; was missing.
(define (degenerate-reason position atom v)
  ;; STRUCTURAL, not textual.  The first version asked whether the wire
  ;; contained a dot, which caught the empty and proper lists and missed
  ;; the dotted pair -- `(a . (b . c))` writes as `(a b . c)`, dot and
  ;; all, while being the same datum as a two-element improper list.
  ;; Scheme's representation hides that; the JavaScript model, where a
  ;; dotted list is items-plus-tail, showed it immediately.
  ;;
  ;; The true rule is the format's: a tail that is itself a pair or the
  ;; empty list MERGES into the enclosing list.  So the tail position
  ;; exists only for atoms that are neither.
  (and (string=? position "as-tail")
       (let ((tail (cdr v)))
         (and (or (null? tail) (pair? tail))
              (string-append
               "the format has no such datum: a tail that is itself a list "
               "or a pair merges into the enclosing list -- (a . (b ...)) "
               "IS (a b ...) -- so `" atom "` cannot occupy a tail "
               "position distinguishably")))))

(define nesting-holes '())

(define (anchor-records)
  ;; Cases and records walked together: the degeneracy test needs the
  ;; VALUE the cell was built from, and recovering it by index from a
  ;; parallel list is the kind of bookkeeping that goes wrong quietly.
  (let* ((cases (anchor-cases))
         (rs (let sift ((cs cases) (keep '()) (holes '()))
               (if (null? cs)
                   (begin (set! nesting-holes (reverse holes))
                          (reverse keep))
                   (let* ((c (car cs))
                          (v (caddr c))
                          (position (and (> (length c) 5) (list-ref c 5)))
                          (atom (and (> (length c) 4) (list-ref c 4)))
                          (why (and position
                                    (degenerate-reason position atom v))))
                     (if why
                         (sift (cdr cs) keep
                               (cons (list atom position why) holes))
                         (sift (cdr cs)
                               (cons (anchor-record c) keep)
                               holes)))))))
    ;; A product can have degenerate cells -- consing the empty list as a
    ;; tail gives back a proper list -- and a cell that is a duplicate of
    ;; another anchor looks like coverage while adding none.  Names must
    ;; be distinct for the same reason: two rows under one name is a
    ;; branch that silently replaced another.
    (let dup ((xs rs))
      (unless (null? xs)
        (let ((here (car xs)))
          (for-each
           (lambda (other)
             (when (string=? (string-append (car here) "/" (cadr here))
                             (string-append (car other) "/" (cadr other)))
               (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                      (string-append "two anchors named "
                                     (car here) "/" (cadr here))))
             ;; The VALUE each spec denotes, not the spec text.  Two
             ;; different specs can denote one datum -- Q 710 226 and
             ;; Q 355 113 are both 355/113, P 0 X is X -- so comparing
             ;; the text lets a semantic duplicate through, which is a
             ;; row that looks like coverage and is none.
             (when (and (same-datum? (spec->value (caddr here))
                                     (spec->value (caddr other)))
                        (string=? (cadddr here) (cadddr other)))
               (error 'gen-sexpr-vectors
                      (string-append "these two anchors are the same value "
                                     "written the same way, so one of them "
                                     "is coverage that is not there: "
                                     (car here) "/" (cadr here) " and "
                                     (car other) "/" (cadr other)))))
           (cdr xs))
          (dup (cdr xs)))))
    rs))

;; ---- run the oracle ---------------------------------------------------

;; the position out of a "message @pos" rendering, as a number, so the
;; downstream suites can compare it instead of only noticing that
;; something raised
(define (cddr-pos msg)
  (let loop ((i (- (string-length msg) 1)))
    (cond ((< i 0) -1)
          ((char=? (string-ref msg i) #\@)
           (or (string->number (substring msg (+ i 1) (string-length msg))) -1))
          (else (loop (- i 1))))))

(define (err->string e)
  (if (and (vector? e) (= (vector-length e) 3))
      (format "~a @~a" (vector-ref e 1) (vector-ref e 2))
      (format "~a" e)))

;; Parsing and re-writing are recorded SEPARATELY.  Folding them into
;; one guard reports "rejected" for an input the reader happily accepts
;; but the writer will not emit again (a symbol named "+1" is exactly
;; that), which would teach the other implementations to reject an
;; input the authority accepts.
(define (probe input)
  ;; -> (parsed? . (parse-error | (rewritable? . (canonical | write-error))))
  (guard (e (#t (cons #f (err->string e))))
    (let ((v (string->sexpr-extended input)))
      (cons #t
            (guard (e2 (#t (cons #f (err->string e2))))
              (cons #t (sexpr->string-extended v)))))))

(define (main out-path)
  (call-with-output-file out-path
    (lambda (p)
      (put-string p "{\n  \"generator\": ")
      (json-string "test/gen-sexpr-vectors.sc" p)
      (put-string p ",\n  \"authority\": ")
      (json-string "(igropyr sexpr)" p)
      (put-string p ",\n  \"authority_commit\": ")
      (json-string (authority-commit) p)
      (put-string p ",\n  \"authority_commits\": {")
      (let loop ((fs measured-sources) (first #t))
        (unless (null? fs)
          (unless first (put-string p ", "))
          (json-string (car fs) p)
          (put-string p ": ")
          (json-string (recorded-commit (car fs)) p)
          (loop (cdr fs) #f)))
      (put-string p "}")
      (put-string p ",\n  \"authority_commit_is\": ")
      (json-string "the last commit touching sexpr.sc, not the checkout's HEAD" p)
      (put-string p ",\n  \"counts\": {\"write\": ")
      (put-string p (number->string (length (append write-cases extra-write-cases))))
      (put-string p ", \"read\": ")
      (put-string p (number->string (length read-cases)))
      (put-string p ", \"write_reject\": ")
      (put-string p (number->string (length write-reject-cases)))
      (put-string p ", \"anchors\": ")
      (put-string p (number->string (length (anchor-records))))
      (put-string p "}")
      (put-string p ",\n  \"entry_points\": ")
      (json-string "string->sexpr-extended / sexpr->string-extended" p)
      (put-string p ",\n  \"note\": ")
      (json-string
       (string-append
        "Generated by test/gen-sexpr-vectors.sc from (igropyr sexpr) -- "
        "the authority for this wire format.  Do not hand-edit.  The "
        "read probes' inputs are chosen by the generator; every VERDICT "
        "here, and every wire string in the write groups, is what that "
        "implementation produced. Wire text is base64 of its UTF-8 bytes, "
        "except where an entry is too long to carry literally: those "
        "appear as a *_rule object (prefix, repeated character, count, "
        "suffix) that rebuilds the text exactly.")
       p)
      (put-string p ",\n  \"write\": [\n")
      (let loop ((cs (append write-cases extra-write-cases)) (first #t))
        (unless (null? cs)
          (let* ((c (car cs))
                 (name (car c))
                 (kind (cadr c))
                 (v (caddr c))
                 (wire (sexpr->string-extended v)))
            (unless first (put-string p ",\n"))
            (put-string p "    {\"name\": ")
            (json-string name p)
            (put-string p ", \"kind\": ")
            (json-string kind p)
            ;; A vector whose wire runs to tens of kilobytes is stored
            ;; as the RULE that describes it rather than as the bytes.
            ;; The oracle still produced those bytes here; what holds
            ;; the rule to them is downstream, in the suite that builds
            ;; the value by hand and writes it against the rebuilt wire
            ;; (see the note on wire-rule).  No byte goes unverified,
            ;; but not by anything on this line.
            (let ((rule (wire-rule wire)))
              (if rule
                  (begin
                    (put-string p ", \"wire_rule\": {\"prefix\": ")
                    (json-string (car rule) p)
                    (put-string p ", \"repeat\": ")
                    (json-string (cadr rule) p)
                    (put-string p ", \"count\": ")
                    (put-string p (number->string (caddr rule)))
                    (put-string p ", \"suffix\": ")
                    (json-string (cadddr rule) p)
                    (put-string p "}"))
                  (begin
                    (put-string p ", \"wire_b64\": ")
                    (json-string (b64 wire) p))))
            (put-string p "}")
            (loop (cdr cs) #f))))
      (put-string p "\n  ],\n  \"read\": [\n")
      (let loop ((cs read-cases) (first #t))
        (unless (null? cs)
          (let* ((c (car cs))
                 (name (car c))
                 (input (cadr c))
                 (r (probe input)))
            (unless first (put-string p ",\n"))
            (put-string p "    {\"name\": ")
            (json-string name p)
            (let ((rule (wire-rule input)))
              (if rule
                  (begin
                    (put-string p ", \"input_rule\": {\"prefix\": ")
                    (json-string (car rule) p)
                    (put-string p ", \"repeat\": ")
                    (json-string (cadr rule) p)
                    (put-string p ", \"count\": ")
                    (put-string p (number->string (caddr rule)))
                    (put-string p ", \"suffix\": ")
                    (json-string (cadddr rule) p)
                    (put-string p "}"))
                  (begin
                    (put-string p ", \"input_b64\": ")
                    (json-string (b64 input) p))))
            (if (car r)
                (let ((w (cdr r)))
                  (put-string p ", \"accepted\": true")
                  (if (car w)
                      (let ((crule (wire-rule (cdr w))))
                        (if crule
                            (begin
                              (put-string p ", \"rewritable\": true, \"canonical_rule\": {\"prefix\": ")
                              (json-string (car crule) p)
                              (put-string p ", \"repeat\": ")
                              (json-string (cadr crule) p)
                              (put-string p ", \"count\": ")
                              (put-string p (number->string (caddr crule)))
                              (put-string p ", \"suffix\": ")
                              (json-string (cadddr crule) p)
                              (put-string p "}"))
                            (begin
                              (put-string p ", \"rewritable\": true, \"canonical_b64\": ")
                              (json-string (b64 (cdr w)) p))))
                      (begin
                        (put-string p ", \"rewritable\": false, \"write_error\": ")
                        (json-string (cdr w) p))))
                (begin
                  (put-string p ", \"accepted\": false, \"error\": ")
                  (json-string (cdr r) p)
                  (put-string p ", \"error_pos\": ")
                  (put-string p (number->string (cddr-pos (cdr r))))))
            (put-string p "}")
            (loop (cdr cs) #f))))
      (put-string p "\n  ],\n  \"write_reject\": [\n")
      (let loop ((cs write-reject-cases) (first #t))
        (unless (null? cs)
          (let* ((c (car cs))
                 (name (car c))
                 (text (cadr c))
                 (r (guard (e (#t (cons 'rejected
                                        (if (and (vector? e) (= (vector-length e) 3))
                                            (format "~a" (vector-ref e 1))
                                            (format "~a" e)))))
                      (cons 'accepted
                            (sexpr->string-extended (string->symbol text))))))
            (unless first (put-string p ",\n"))
            (put-string p "    {\"name\": ")
            (json-string name p)
            (put-string p ", \"sym\": ")
            (json-string text p)
            ;; If the authority ever again writes a name its own reader
            ;; refuses, that shows up here as a marked entry rather than
            ;; as a downstream mystery.  It currently never does.
            (when (eq? (car r) 'accepted)
              (let ((back (guard (e (#t 'raised))
                            (string->sexpr-extended (cdr r)))))
                (unless (and (symbol? back)
                             (string=? (symbol->string back) text))
                  (put-string p ", \"divergence\": ")
                  (json-string
                   (string-append
                    "the authority writes this name, and reading that wire "
                    "back gives "
                    (if (eq? back 'raised) "an error" "a different datum"))
                   p))))
            (if (eq? (car r) 'accepted)
                (begin
                  (put-string p ", \"rejected\": false, \"wire_b64\": ")
                  (json-string (b64 (cdr r)) p))
                (begin
                  (put-string p ", \"rejected\": true, \"error\": ")
                  (json-string (cdr r) p)))
            (put-string p "}")
            (loop (cdr cs) #f))))
      (put-string p "\n  ],\n  \"anchors_are\": ")
      (json-string
       (string-append
        "type x spelling-branch over the whole value model.  Each entry "
        "carries a spec -- a prefix token program whose payloads are all "
        "decimal integers and whose text is UTF-8 bytes -- and the wire "
        "the authority produces for the value that spec denotes.  A "
        "consumer builds the value from the spec WITHOUT its own codec "
        "and asserts both directions against the wire; that is what a "
        "fixed-point round trip cannot ask.  direction=both means the "
        "authority also emits this exact wire for the value; "
        "direction=read means the wire is an accepted spelling the "
        "writer does not itself produce.  Downstream tests are expected "
        "to pin the branch names, so a branch cannot quietly leave.  "
        "Every entry carries the atom its value is, and a nesting entry "
        "also the position it places it in, so a branch name is a claim "
        "checked against the decoded value as far as the label reaches. "
        "It does not reach everywhere: where two branches share a label "
        "(integer/positive and integer/negative are both small-integer, "
        "flonum/half and flonum/repeating both flonum) their names are "
        "conventions, and swapping such a pair's values and wires would "
        "not be detected.  "
        "The section is additive: a consumer that does not want it can "
        "ignore it.")
       p)
      ;; The spec language's shared corpus, EMITTED rather than copied.
      ;; It was three hand-kept lists in three languages, and the last
      ;; round shipped with two of them extended and the third left at
      ;; its old contents while the report said "one shared list, all
      ;; three".  Three copies cannot be kept in step by discipline;
      ;; there is now one list, and the consumers read it from here.
      (put-string p ",\n  \"spec_corpus_is\": ")
      (json-string
       (string-append
        "Programs in the spec language that every reader of it must "
        "refuse (malformed) and must accept (wellformed).  The generator "
        "runs both against its own reader before writing this file; the "
        "consumers run both against theirs.  The wellformed list is not "
        "decoration: a reader that refused everything would satisfy the "
        "malformed list perfectly.")
       p)
      (put-string p ",\n  \"spec_malformed\": [")
      (let loop ((xs malformed-specs) (first #t))
        (unless (null? xs)
          (unless first (put-string p ", "))
          (json-string (car xs) p)
          (loop (cdr xs) #f)))
      (put-string p "]")
      (put-string p ",\n  \"spec_wellformed\": [\n")
      (let loop ((xs wellformed-specs) (first #t))
        (unless (null? xs)
          (unless first (put-string p ",\n"))
          (put-string p "    {\"spec\": ")
          (json-string (wellformed-program (car xs)) p)
          (put-string p ", \"why\": ")
          (json-string (wellformed-why (car xs)) p)
          (put-string p ", \"wire_b64\": ")
          ;; the authority's bytes for the value this program denotes,
          ;; so "accepted" is no longer the whole assertion
          (json-string (b64 (sexpr->string-extended
                             (spec->value (wellformed-program (car xs)))))
                       p)
          (put-string p "}")
          (loop (cdr xs) #f)))
      (put-string p "\n  ]")
      (put-string p ",\n  \"nesting_holes\": [")
      (let loop ((xs nesting-holes) (first #t))
        (unless (null? xs)
          (unless first (put-string p ", "))
          (put-string p "{\"atom\": ")
          (json-string (car (car xs)) p)
          (put-string p ", \"position\": ")
          (json-string (cadr (car xs)) p)
          (put-string p ", \"reason\": ")
          (json-string (caddr (car xs)) p)
          (put-string p "}")
          (loop (cdr xs) #f)))
      (put-string p "]")
      (put-string p ",\n  \"anchors\": [\n")
      (let loop ((cs (anchor-records)) (first #t))
        (unless (null? cs)
          (let ((c (car cs)))
            (unless first (put-string p ",\n"))
            (put-string p "    {\"type\": ")
            (json-string (car c) p)
            (put-string p ", \"branch\": ")
            (json-string (cadr c) p)
            (put-string p ", \"spec\": ")
            (json-string (caddr c) p)
            (put-string p ", \"wire_b64\": ")
            (json-string (b64 (cadddr c)) p)
            (put-string p ", \"direction\": ")
            (json-string (car (cddddr c)) p)
            ;; Every anchor says what its value IS; a nesting cell also
            ;; says where it puts it.  Both are data a consumer checks
            ;; against the decoded value, so a branch name is a claim
            ;; checked as far as the label reaches -- and no further:
            ;; where two branches share a label (integer/positive and
            ;; integer/negative are both small-integer) their names are
            ;; conventions, and swapping such a pair's values and wires
            ;; would not be detected.  See the note this emits.
            (put-string p ", \"atom\": ")
            (json-string (list-ref c 5) p)
            (when (list-ref c 6)
              (put-string p ", \"position\": ")
              (json-string (list-ref c 6) p))
            (put-string p "}")
            (loop (cdr cs) #f))))
      (put-string p "\n  ]\n}\n"))
    'truncate))

(library-path-is-here!)
(authority-clean!)
(resolution-matches-guards!)
(sentinel!)
(spec-readers-agree!)
(sources-unchanged!)
;; Write to a temporary file, re-check, and only then put it in place.
;; The check used to run after main had already written the real file,
;; so it aborted saying "nothing written" while the fixture sat there
;; -- the guard fired and the message was false.
(let* ((out (car (command-line-arguments)))
       (tmp (string-append out ".tmp")))
  (main tmp)
  (guard (e (#t (when (file-exists? tmp) (delete-file tmp)) (raise e)))
    (sources-unchanged!))
  (when (file-exists? out) (delete-file out))
  (rename-file tmp out))
